<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<module fritzingVersion="1.0.2" moduleId="makerbuddy-kit-lcd-1602-i2c-r3" referenceFile="lcd-1602-i2c.fzp">
 <version>1</version>
 <author>MakerBuddy (https://makerbuddy.cc)</author>
 <title>LCD 16x2 with I2C backpack with its cable (MakerBuddy)</title>
 <label>LCD</label>
 <date>2026-11-24</date>
 <url>https://github.com/MakerBuddy/MakerBuddy-IoT-Kit-Fritzing-Parts</url>
 <tags>
  <tag>MakerBuddy</tag>
  <tag>IoT Kit</tag>
  <tag>LCD</tag>
  <tag>1602</tag>
  <tag>16x2</tag>
  <tag>I2C</tag>
  <tag>PCF8574</tag>
  <tag>HD44780</tag>
  <tag>display</tag>
 </tags>
 <properties>
  <property name="family">MakerBuddy character LCD</property>
  <property name="type">character LCD</property>
  <property name="characters">16 x 2</property>
  <property name="interface">I2C (PCF8574, 0x27)</property>
  <property name="connector">JST-XH 4-pin</property>
  <property name="board connector">I2C</property>
  <property name="cable">kit cable, flexible</property>
 </properties>
 <description>16 x 2 character LCD (HD44780) with a PCF8574 I2C backpack at address 0x27 and the MakerBuddy JST adapter on its left edge. Four wires: VCC, GND, SCL, SDA. Plugs into any of the three I2C positions of the MakerBuddy IoT Kit board. The order of the four joints on the adapter tab is taken from the standard backpack (GND, VCC, SDA, SCL from the top); the names are what matter in Fritzing. This part comes with the kit's 4-wire cable. Drop it with the cable's ends on the I2C connector of the MakerBuddy IoT Kit board and all 4 wires connect; then hold Alt (Option) and drag the part to move it while the cable stays plugged in. Each wire end can also be dragged to a pin on its own. https://makerbuddy.cc</description>
